><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-16.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>ACL injuries result in considerable time loss from sport in the Australian High Performance Sport System, with one in four athletes reported to experience at least one further subsequent ACL injury. A higher proportion of knee injuries were ACLs in female athletes compared to male athletes, and a reduction in time to RTS was observed with increasing athlete age. Effective primary prevention, rehabilitation processes, and ongoing tertiary prevention strategies that mitigate against subsequent ACL injuries are warranted in this Australian athlete population.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="4" rect="595.276001,149.257187,779.276001,241.257187"
/></highlight
